Before the confetti of ashen leaves
is cleared by a groundskeeper,
a white squirrel erases two blueberries
from the palm of a tourist.

Before the domestic mouse 
of my migrant body can retreat
into the familiar hole of my lodging,
seven yellow-billed ducks
paddle out of the pond 
and gather on the lawn, 
waiting for a blanket
of snow.

Tourists feed pigeons from their hands.
The stranger they have just ignored
watches, jealous of the pigeons.
 

The cold pond.
The lawn.
Eight homeless ducks.
